y exclude it."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would the absence of jaundice or pruritus in this presentation make biliary obstruction less likely than acute hepatic vein thrombosis?", "summary": "Jaundice is a hallmark of biliary obstruction (B3), but its absence doesn't rule it out. Hepatic vein thrombosis (B2) can also present without jaundice in early stages. This weakly argues against B3 compared to B2."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the RUQ localization of pain inconsistent with acute pancreatitis, which typically presents with epigastric radiation?", "summary": "Acute pancreatitis typically presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back. RUQ localization is atypical and suggests a strong argument against B4 as the primary diagnosis."}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "The patient exhibits hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) consistent with shock, requiring immediate stabilization before diagnostic resolution.", "required_actions": ["Administer intravenous fluids for hypotension", "Prepare for emergent imaging (e.g., ultrasound) to confirm ectopic pregnancy or other acute abdomen causes"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Pending urinary β-hCG results and unresolved dangerous alternatives (B1.1: 0.8 danger, B1.2: 0.7 danger) require further evaluation before committing to a diagnosis. Emergency actions (fluids, imaging prep) are already in progress, but tree expansion should continue to refine the differential."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.95, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.46196699927519447, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation o